I have exact lines. Key facts for the finding:

- `fs/ceph/snap.c:848-849`: `num_split_inos = le32_to_cpu(h->num_split_inos); num_split_realms = le32_to_cpu(h->num_split_realms);` — raw 32-bit wire counts from the MDS peer (ceph metadata server), a remote peer of a network filesystem protocol. No range check against any maximum. The only message-level check is `msg->front.iov_len < sizeof(*h)` (line 842).

- Pointer advance `p += sizeof(u64) * num_split_inos` (873) and `p += sizeof(u64) * num_split_realms` (875) — `sizeof(u64)` is `size_t` (unsigned long, 64-bit) so no 32-bit multiply overflow; and `ceph_decode_need(&p, e, sizeof(*ri), bad)` (876) validates that p is still in bounds after the advances. So the pointer walk is validated, and the loops at 890 (`for (i = 0; i < num_split_inos; i++) ... le64_to_cpu(split_inos[i])`) and 952 (`num_split_realms`) iterate within the checked message region... wait — actually does ceph_decode_need check that p + sizeof(*ri) <= e, i.e., it validates only that the END of both arrays plus the realm info struct is inside the message. The loops iterate up to num_split_inos over split_inos[i], which is within the region p advanced over. Since p advanced by exactly num_split_inos*8 and ceph_decode_need confirmed p+sizeof(*ri) <= e, all split_inos[i] for i < num_split_inos are within the message. So actually ceph handle_snap is safe (validated via ceph_decode_need). The loop reads are in-bounds. So ceph is safe. Good — classify safe.

Wait, one nuance: `ceph_decode_need(&p, e, sizeof(*ri), bad)` — checks p + sizeof(*ri) <= e. p at that point = msg start + sizeof(*h) + 8*num_split_inos + 8*num_split_realms. So yes all array elements are within [msg start, p), which is <= e. Safe. And sizeof(u64) is size_t so no overflow in pointer arithmetic (though pointer overflow would be caught anyway by the need check? If the multiply overflowed 64-bit... u32 count * 8 can't overflow 64-bit. Safe).

Now the LE advertising report loops in hci_event.c:

`hci_le_adv_report_evt` (line 5344):
```
